"""Mentor-specified front-half prose; metric placeholders filled from frozen summaries."""
TITLE='Source-Anchored LMMSE Estimation for MIMO-OFDM with Cross-Pilot Fitting and Low-Rank Corrections'
ABSTRACT=r'''Source-statistics mismatch can degrade linear minimum mean-square error (LMMSE) channel estimation when sparse pilots provide insufficient information to estimate a target covariance and no target-condition label is available online. We study a frozen Source empirical LMMSE estimator augmented by prepared statistical correction directions. Four-fold cross-pilot fitting over a current-inclusive five-frame causal window determines two unrestricted real coordinates; only the deployed corrections are compressed to rank four. In the formal composed-shift evaluation at CDL-B, 120~km/h and 100~ns delay spread, the rank-four estimator reduces pooled-energy normalized mean-square error by {source10} and {source20}~dB relative to Source, and by {convex10} and {convex20}~dB relative to the evaluated convex combination, at 10 and 20~dB signal-to-noise ratio, respectively. Its dense-to-rank-four penalties are {penalty10} and {penalty20}~dB, meeting the preregistered {margin}-dB criterion, while the specified arithmetic proxy is {saving}\% lower than that of the dense affine implementation. Independent sealed validation at CDL-A, 120~km/h and 100~ns yields Source-relative reductions of {sealed10} and {sealed20}~dB at the same signal-to-noise ratios, for a single independently evaluated unseen-profile condition. In-distribution degradation, condition-dependent compression effects and a remaining gap to the finite matched-statistics reference delimit this evidence: adaptation within the prepared correction space provides partial compensation rather than a guarantee across arbitrary channel conditions.'''

CONTRIBUTIONS=[
 r'We study pilot-only adaptation of a frozen empirical LMMSE estimator through correction directions prepared from labeled channel-statistics conditions, with the Source operator retained as the additive base.',
 r'For this prepared correction space, we formulate four-fold cross-pilot fitting over a current-inclusive five-frame causal window and obtain two unrestricted real coordinates through a small analytical normal-equation solve.',
 r'We preserve the full Source operator and compress only the deployed corrections to rank four; the dense and compressed variants share the online coordinates and retain dense pilot predictors.',
 r'We evaluate the frozen receiver using a formal test and an independent sealed unseen-profile validation, and use development sensitivity studies and post-hoc receiver diagnostics to describe the statistical-budget, pilot-geometry and receiver-scope limitations.']

INTRO_PARAGRAPHS=[
 ('I01','problem',r'''Statistical channel estimation uses received pilot symbols to infer channel coefficients on resource elements that carry data. In multiple-input multiple-output orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (MIMO-OFDM), the relation between these observed and unobserved coefficients depends on spatial and time-frequency channel statistics. An empirical linear minimum mean-square error (LMMSE) estimator therefore combines a particular statistical preparation with the receiver's pilot geometry and noise variance. A filter prepared for a source condition can become mismatched when the channel profile or mobility changes. Increasing the pilot density or acquiring new target-condition statistics could alter that problem, but neither option belongs to the fixed online information budget studied here.'''),
 ('I02','existing solutions',r'''Observation-adaptive channel estimation already encompasses several interfaces. Candidate correlation selection uses prediction residuals to choose statistical parameters~\cite{mei2021}; Gaussian mixture models (GMMs) and mixtures of factor analyzers (MFAs) use observation-dependent posterior responsibilities to combine conditional LMMSE estimates~\cite{koller2022gmm,fesl2022structured,fesl2023mfa}. Adaptive low-rank MIMO-OFDM LMMSE estimation also predates this work~\cite{ozdemir2005,ozdemir2006}. Learned estimators provide further options, including an offline-trained filter with linear inference~\cite{ha2026mmse}. These approaches establish that adaptation from observations and compact statistical representations are available principles. They motivate a comparison of the statistical objects, adapted variables and information interfaces, rather than a claim that observation-adaptive estimation is missing.'''),
 ('I03','question and hypothesis',r'''We ask whether a limited space of corrections prepared offline can partially compensate statistical drift when its coordinates are estimated from the received sparse pilots alone. The hypothesis is deliberately finite: selected prepared conditions may provide useful correction directions for another channel condition, without spanning its full statistical estimator. Its value must therefore be assessed both by improvement over the frozen Source estimator and by the remaining error relative to a matched-statistics reference. The question also separates two operations that are easily conflated: preparing a correction library from clean channel samples offline, and selecting its online coordinates without access to a clean target channel.'''),
 ('I04','source-anchored interface',r'''The resulting interface is source-anchored: the final estimator retains the frozen Source empirical LMMSE operator as its additive base. This term describes the implemented operator structure; it does not invoke a Bayesian source-domain adaptation theorem. The prepared directions are differences between empirical LMMSE operators for the Source and two preparation conditions. We refer to them as the profile-origin correction and the mobility-origin correction, according to their preparation provenance. These names do not identify independent physical channel factors. Two real coefficients weight the directions at deployment, allowing the estimate to move within a prescribed affine space while retaining the Source centering and base operator.'''),
 ('I05','cross-pilot definition',r'''Cross-pilot fitting is defined here as predicting the observations in one of four deterministic pilot folds from the other three folds, and fitting the correction coordinates to the resulting prediction residuals. The folds partition the existing pilots and preserve the frozen spatial and time-frequency geometry; they introduce no additional transmitted pilots. Their objectives are accumulated over five consecutive frames, including the current frame and excluding future frames. A small real normal-equation solve yields two unrestricted coordinates, with no clipping, probability-simplex projection or ridge penalty. Windowed affine least squares has prior art~\cite{azpicueta2010ls}; the formulation studied here applies it to this prepared statistical correction space and cross-pilot objective.'''),
 ('I06','compression and information contract',r'''Rank reduction is applied only to the deployed correction operators. Each correction uses rank-four factors, whereas the full Source operator and the pilot prediction operators used for coordinate fitting are retained. The dense and compressed variants consequently use the same online coordinates, making their comparison specific to the deployment approximation. Offline preparation requires clean channel statistics from labeled preparation conditions. Online operation uses received pilot observations, known pilot geometry and noise variance, and the frozen library; it requires no target clean channel state information (CSI), target covariance or target environment label. This distinction makes the offline acquisition assumption explicit rather than treating pilot-only operation as a data-free estimator.'''),
 ('I07','surrogate and theory boundary',r'''The fitting objective concerns prediction of noisy pilot observations, while the reported channel-estimation error is measured on data resource elements. A coordinate that minimizes the former need not minimize the latter. Moreover, the prediction columns themselves depend on received observations, so an ordinary independent-noise regression interpretation cannot be assumed. The link between pilot fitting and data-domain risk is therefore assessed empirically under the stated protocol and conditionally within the exact estimator equations. It is not a universal optimality guarantee, and the origin labels of the corrections do not provide a physical decomposition of the target channel.'''),
 ('I08','evidence overview',r'''The evaluation separates a formal test on specified source and shifted conditions from an independent sealed validation on an unseen CDL-A profile condition. The formal test supports Source-relative and evaluated convex-combination comparisons for the composed shift, together with a preregistered compression criterion. The independent validation tests the frozen receiver at one additional profile, speed and delay-spread combination. Separate development studies indicate sensitivity to offline statistical budgets, subset realizations and pilot geometry; they are not additional formal replications. Post-hoc receiver diagnostics describe how estimation and detection metrics can differ for the fixed receiver, without serving as primary evidence or establishing a causal mechanism.'''),
 ('I09','boundary and scope',r'''The scope includes in-distribution degradation, condition-dependent compression effects, a delay-spread boundary and a finite gap to the matched-statistics reference. That reference is prepared from a finite target-condition sample set and is not a population MMSE oracle. Mobility evaluation reflects time-varying channel statistics under a symbol-wise quasi-static abstraction: it does not validate full doubly-selective inter-carrier interference (ICI) robustness. The tested unseen-profile condition likewise does not establish arbitrary-channel generalization. These boundaries frame the contribution as a measured tradeoff for a specified statistical library and pilot interface, rather than an unrestricted replacement for matched statistical estimation.''')]

INTRO_END=r'''The contributions are as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
{contributions}
\end{enumerate}
The remainder of the paper reviews related estimation interfaces, specifies the system and prepared corrections, describes the frozen evaluation protocol, and reports the estimation, compression and implementation tradeoffs before discussing their limitations.'''

RELATED_PARAGRAPHS=[
 ('RW1','Classical, adaptive and reduced-rank LMMSE',r'''Classical LMMSE estimation uses channel statistics and an observation model to construct a linear conditional estimator, as recalled in the statistical formulation of Koller et al.~\cite{koller2022gmm}. Its usefulness and computational cost depend on how the statistics are represented and how observed coefficients constrain unobserved ones. Adaptive low-rank LMMSE estimation for MIMO-OFDM was studied previously by \"Ozdemir, Arslan and Arvas~\cite{ozdemir2005,ozdemir2006}, and the original abstract of their 2006 paper explicitly discusses comb-type pilots and subspace tracking~\cite{ozdemir2006}. The available public sources did not permit a full method-field audit of these works, so we make no priority claim over their adaptation variables or rank construction. For the present estimator, rank reduction is specified on the prepared correction operators, while the full Source base and pilot predictors remain unchanged. This defines our compression object without inferring a contrasting object for the inaccessible methods.'''),
 ('RW2','Affine combinations of adaptive filters',r'''Affine combinations of adaptive filters provide a direct mathematical precedent for continuous estimator coordinates. Azpicueta-Ruiz et al. formulate least-squares adaptation of multiple filter outputs with a sum-to-one affine constraint, retain independent coordinates that may fall outside the probability simplex, and use temporal weighting windows~\cite{azpicueta2010ls}. Their reference-output-plus-differences parameterization and normal equations also precede the algebra used here~\cite{azpicueta2010ls}. The separate author preprint of Gui and Xu studies affine combinations of adaptive sparse LMS filters~\cite{gui2014preprint}, and the four-author journal paper studies sparse LMS combinations for large-scale MIMO channel estimation~\cite{gui2015journal}. We do not assume that the two versions have identical equations or constraints. Thus affine combination, unrestricted coordinates and windowed least squares are established components~\cite{azpicueta2010ls}. The interface examined here fixes the empirical LMMSE correction operators after statistical preparation and fits their coordinates through cross-pilot prediction. The distinction concerns those prepared objects and the observation objective, rather than the general affine-combination principle. '''),
 ('RW3','GMM, structured GMM and MFA',r'''GMM estimators learn statistical channel mixtures offline and combine conditional LMMSE estimates using observation-dependent posterior responsibilities~\cite{koller2022gmm}. Fesl et al. extend this approach with structured covariances and time-frequency or cascaded processing~\cite{fesl2022structured}. Fesl, Turan and Utschick use mixtures of factor analyzers to represent component statistics through low-rank loadings together with a residual covariance term~\cite{fesl2023mfa}; a low-rank loading does not require the complete component covariance to be low rank~\cite{fesl2023mfa}. These works already establish offline statistical learning, observation-dependent mixing, conditional LMMSE estimators and compact statistical representations~\cite{koller2022gmm,fesl2022structured,fesl2023mfa}. Their component means and posterior probability weights are part of the estimator, with nonnegative responsibilities summing to one~\cite{koller2022gmm,fesl2022structured,fesl2023mfa}. Choosing a reference component can rewrite such a mixture algebraically as a base plus differences, so that rewriting alone does not distinguish the present method. Our specified interface instead retains the Source centering and operator, fits unrestricted real correction coordinates by cross-pilot prediction, and compresses only the deployed statistical differences. GMM and MFA remain closest statistical comparators; these interface distinctions do not establish performance superiority.'''),
 ('RW4','Candidate and parameter selection',r'''Candidate selection provides a closely related pilot-based objective. Mei et al. compare candidate channel correlation functions using prediction residuals calculated from noisy rough channel estimates~\cite{mei2021}. In their estimator, the prediction of a component excludes that component, and the resulting leave-one-out errors determine a discrete candidate selection~\cite{mei2021}. This establishes an observation-based statistical selection procedure without requiring clean target channels for the selection rule~\cite{mei2021}. The present cross-pilot objective therefore does not introduce noisy pilot prediction as an isolated principle. It fits a continuous pair of unrestricted coordinates rather than selecting one correlation candidate, and it uses four deterministic folds over a current-inclusive five-frame window for the frozen prepared correction space. A discrete selector, a posterior probability mixture and an unrestricted affine fit can all be expressed as combinations of estimator outputs, but their allowable coefficients and decision objectives differ~\cite{mei2021,koller2022gmm,azpicueta2010ls}. Those differences specify the interfaces being compared and do not imply that one type of adaptation is preferable in every pilot geometry or channel distribution.'''),
 ('RW5','Learned linear and neural estimation',r'''Learned channel estimators offer another separation between preparation and inference. The inspected arXiv v5 manuscript of Ha et al. uses an attention-based training construction that yields an MMSE filter with linear inference~\cite{ha2026mmse}. That manuscript is cited as a versioned preprint rather than assigned an unverified journal publication status. Channelformer is an attention-based neural channel estimator~\cite{luan2023channelformer}. Our offline operators are empirically estimated from clean channel statistics, and their online adaptation consists of the analytical cross-pilot coordinate solve rather than neural inference or neural parameter updates. A faithful project-scale numerical comparison with the examined public MFA and A-MMSE implementations was not established under our fixed reproduction protocol; accordingly, we make no performance claim relative to those methods. '''),
 ('RW6','Positioning of this study',r'''These lines of work motivate separating the statistical objects prepared offline, the variables adapted online, the fitting objective, the temporal information interface, and the location of rank reduction. The comparison above identifies precedents for conditional statistical mixtures, affine filter combinations and prediction-residual selection~\cite{koller2022gmm,fesl2023mfa,azpicueta2010ls,mei2021}. We study their relevant components within a specified Source-anchored prepared-correction interface: a full frozen Source empirical LMMSE base, profile-origin and mobility-origin statistical differences, two unrestricted real coordinates from four-fold cross-pilot fitting over five causal frames, and rank-four deployment of the corrections alone. The prepared library requires clean offline statistics from labeled conditions; its online coordinate fit uses received pilots without target clean CSI, target covariance or an environment label. The contribution is the formulation and evaluation of this complete interface, with its finite correction space and observed limitations. It does not attribute priority to affine mixing, temporal least squares, adaptive low-rank LMMSE or noisy prediction residuals individually, and it does not resolve the unavailable method fields of the \"Ozdemir works.''')]

